.TH PALADIN 1 "2026-05-31" "paladin 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
paladin \- simple, safe symmetric file encryption
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B paladin
.RB ( \-e | \-\-encrypt " | " \-d | \-\-decrypt " | " \-i | \-\-info " | " \-\-verify )
.IR FILE " [" OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B paladin
encrypts and decrypts files with authenticated symmetric encryption. An
encrypted container begins with a self-describing, authenticated header, so a
file can be decrypted with only the password or keyfile \(em no out-of-band
parameters. The default cipher is AES-256-GCM and the default key-derivation
function is Argon2id.
.PP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-verify ", and " \-i
also read foreign
.B AES Crypt
.RB ( .aes )
files (Stream Format 1 and 2), detected automatically. Encryption always writes
paladin's own format, never AES Crypt; to migrate a
.B .aes
file, decrypt it and re-encrypt with paladin. An AES Crypt header is
unauthenticated, so
.B \-i
labels it
.BR "authenticated: false" .
.PP
Exactly one mode and one
.I FILE
are required (except with
.B \-\-help
or
.BR \-\-version ).
A
.I FILE
of
.B \-
means standard input. Run with no arguments at all,
.B paladin
prints its help text to standard error and exits 2.
.SH MODES
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-encrypt
Encrypt
.I FILE
to an output container.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-decrypt
Decrypt
.IR FILE .
A paladin or AES Crypt
.RB ( .aes )
container is detected automatically.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-info
Print unauthenticated header metadata to standard output. No password is read.
For a paladin file this is the cipher, KDF and parameters, version, flags, chunk
size, and a stored filename when present and well-formed; for an AES Crypt file
it is the version, KDF, extension count, and
.B CREATED_BY
value, ending with
.BR "authenticated: false" .
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Decrypt and discard the output to verify integrity and the secret (paladin or
AES Crypt). Nothing is written; exit status 0 means valid.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-output " FILE"
Output path;
.B \-
means standard output. Defaults are described under OUTPUT DEFAULTS. Applies to
encrypt and decrypt only.
.TP
.BI \-p " PW" "\fR, \fP" \-\-password " PW"
Password supplied inline.
.B Discouraged:
it leaks to process listings and shell history. Prefer an interactive prompt,
.BR \-\-password\-file ,
or
.BR \-\-password\-env .
.TP
.BI \-\-password\-file " FILE"
Read the password from a file. Exactly one trailing LF or CRLF is trimmed. When
decrypting or verifying an AES Crypt
.RB ( .aes )
file the contents must be valid UTF-8 text (this is how an AES Crypt key file may
be supplied); UTF-16/BOM key files are not supported.
.TP
.BI \-\-password\-env " VAR"
Read the password from the environment variable
.IR VAR .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-password
Use an empty password (keyfile-only mode). Valid only together with
.BR \-k .
.TP
.BI \-k " FILE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-keyfile " FILE"
Keyfile material combined with the password source.
.B \-
is rejected. The keyfile must be a regular file of 1 byte to 1 MiB. Applies to
paladin files only; AES Crypt files have no paladin-style keyfile component, so
.B \-k
(or
.BR \-\-no\-password )
against a
.B .aes
file is a usage error.
.TP
.BI \-c " NAME" "\fR, \fP" \-\-cipher " NAME"
Cipher:
.B aes\-256\-gcm
(default) or
.BR chacha20\-poly1305 .
Encrypt only.
.TP
.BI \-\-kdf " NAME"
Key-derivation function:
.B argon2id
(default),
.BR scrypt ,
or
.BR pbkdf2 .
Encrypt only.
.TP
.BI \-\-argon2\-memory " KiB" "\fR, \fP" \-\-argon2\-time " N" "\fR, \fP" \-\-argon2\-parallelism " N"
Argon2id cost parameters. Require
.B \-\-kdf argon2id
or the default KDF.
.TP
.BI \-\-scrypt\-log\-n " N" "\fR, \fP" \-\-scrypt\-r " N" "\fR, \fP" \-\-scrypt\-p " N"
scrypt cost parameters. Require
.BR "\-\-kdf scrypt" .
.TP
.BI \-\-pbkdf2\-iterations " N"
PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 iteration count. Requires
.BR "\-\-kdf pbkdf2" .
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-armor
Write ASCII-armored (base64) output; recommended when standard output is a
terminal. Decrypt, verify, and info auto-detect armor. Encrypt only.
.TP
.B \-\-name
Store the input's basename in the header (off by default; sensitive). Rejected
with stdin input. Encrypt only.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite an existing output file. By default an existing output is refused.
Applies to encrypt and decrypt.
.TP
.B \-\-remove
After a successful operation, best-effort delete the input. This is a plain
delete with no secure erase. Rejected with stdin input. Applies to encrypt and
decrypt.
.TP
.BR \-\-progress ", " \-\-no\-progress
Force the progress bar on or off. The default is automatic: on when standard
error is a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print additional, secret-free diagnostics to standard error.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress progress and status output. Does not suppress primary stdout (such as
.BR \-\-info )
or errors. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-verbose .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version.
.SH PASSWORD INPUT
For modes that need a secret, at most one of
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-\-password\-file ,
.BR \-\-password\-env ,
or
.B \-\-no\-password
may be given. If none is given, paladin prompts interactively with no echo; on
encrypt the prompt is asked twice and must match. A keyfile, if supplied, is
always combined with the password source. The resolved secret must contain at
least one byte; an empty password is accepted only via
.BR \-\-no\-password .
Password text is used as UTF-8 bytes with no normalization.
.SH OUTPUT DEFAULTS
Without
.BR \-o :
encrypt writes
.IR FILE .paladin
(or
.IR FILE .paladin.asc
with
.BR \-\-armor );
decrypt uses the stored filename when present and well-formed, otherwise strips
a recognized
.IR .paladin.asc ", " .paladin ", " .asc ", or " .aes
suffix, otherwise appends
.IR .dec .
An AES Crypt file stores no filename, so decrypt strips its
.I .aes
suffix (or appends
.IR .dec ).
Stdin encrypt and decrypt require
.BR \-o .
File outputs are written to a sibling temporary file (mode 0600 on Unix) and
renamed into place only after success; a failed run leaves no partial output.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
General or I/O error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or argument error.
.TP
.B 3
Authentication failure (wrong password/keyfile or a corrupted/tampered file).
.TP
.B 4
Unsupported, unknown, or malformed format or header.
.TP
.B 130
Canceled by the user (SIGINT).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Help, usage, and error output are colorized only when the destination stream
is a terminal.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, disables all color output.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
When set (and
.B NO_COLOR
is not), forces color output even when the stream is not a terminal.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Encrypt a file, prompting for a password:
.PP
.RS
.EX
paladin \-e report.pdf
.EE
.RE
.PP
Armored output to standard output:
.PP
.RS
.EX
paladin \-e report.pdf \-o \- \-\-armor > out.asc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decrypt, restoring the stored or derived name:
.PP
.RS
.EX
paladin \-d report.pdf.paladin
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keyfile-only encryption:
.PP
.RS
.EX
paladin \-e vault.tar \-k usb.key \-\-no\-password
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decrypt a foreign AES Crypt file (writes
.IR secret.txt ):
.PP
.RS
.EX
paladin \-d secret.txt.aes
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
The project specification in
.B docs/DESIGN.md
(section 6 for the command-line interface).
